1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:03,050
Let managers approve requests.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:04,710
That sounds like a feature.

3
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:07,970
It is also a surprisingly effective way to

4
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:11,210
hide a dozen decisions
inside one short sentence.

5
00:00:11,870 --> 00:00:12,410
Which managers?

6
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:13,610
Which requests?

7
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:16,230
What happens when someone clicks twice?

8
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:19,670
Cursor can help us uncover
those decisions and

9
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:20,490
build the result.

10
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:22,570
Our job is to make sure the choices

11
00:00:22,570 --> 00:00:25,410
match the product we
actually intend to ship.

12
00:00:27,717 --> 00:00:30,737
Today, we will build a decision contract for

13
00:00:30,737 --> 00:00:32,577
a future Operations Hub feature.

14
00:00:33,197 --> 00:00:36,097
We will inspect what
exists, turn an ambiguous

15
00:00:36,097 --> 00:00:38,137
request into observable behavior,

16
00:00:38,877 --> 00:00:41,917
separate a product constraint
from an implementation choice,

17
00:00:42,277 --> 00:00:44,597
and define the evidence
needed for acceptance.

18
00:00:45,297 --> 00:00:47,837
The artifact we finish
is a usable engineering

19
00:00:47,837 --> 00:00:48,277
brief.

20
00:00:48,277 --> 00:00:51,197
The approval endpoint itself
comes later, after its

21
00:00:51,197 --> 00:00:52,077
foundations exist.

22
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:56,070
Think of four responsibilities,

23
00:00:56,790 --> 00:01:00,550
requirements, architecture,
implementation, and acceptance.

24
00:01:01,230 --> 00:01:03,150
One person might hold several of them.

25
00:01:03,670 --> 00:01:05,890
On a team, they may be spread across

26
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:07,210
product and engineering.

27
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:10,610
An agent can contribute
to every responsibility,

28
00:01:11,410 --> 00:01:13,850
contribution and authority
are separate questions.

29
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:17,750
A useful delegation says
which decisions the agent

30
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:20,950
may make, which constraints
it must preserve and

31
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:22,590
when it should bring back a trade-off.

32
00:01:25,117 --> 00:01:27,917
requirements describe the
behavior people need.

33
00:01:28,457 --> 00:01:30,997
In our example, a manager can approve an

34
00:01:30,997 --> 00:01:33,357
open request belonging to the same tenant.

35
00:01:33,897 --> 00:01:37,537
That sentence contains policy,
the role, the starting

36
00:01:37,537 --> 00:01:39,457
state, and the tenant boundary.

37
00:01:40,217 --> 00:01:41,977
We should choose that policy deliberately.

38
00:01:42,777 --> 00:01:45,117
If the agent guesses that
every signed-in person

39
00:01:45,117 --> 00:01:45,977
is a manager,

40
00:01:46,837 --> 00:01:49,277
beautifully organized code
will not rescue the feature.

41
00:01:51,683 --> 00:01:54,743
Architecture connects that
behavior to a design.

42
00:01:55,443 --> 00:01:57,023
Where is authorization enforced?

43
00:01:57,703 --> 00:02:00,123
How does the status change
stay consistent with

44
00:02:00,123 --> 00:02:00,903
its audit record?

45
00:02:01,623 --> 00:02:04,563
An agent can inspect the
project, compare options,

46
00:02:04,823 --> 00:02:05,863
and explain costs.

47
00:02:06,523 --> 00:02:08,923
We can authorize it to
choose within existing

48
00:02:08,923 --> 00:02:09,463
conventions.

49
00:02:10,323 --> 00:02:12,503
A new service or a new database is

50
00:02:12,503 --> 00:02:15,023
a larger decision because it changes what we

51
00:02:15,023 --> 00:02:16,403
must operate and maintain.

52
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:22,883
Implementation is where delegation
can become very concrete.

53
00:02:23,743 --> 00:02:26,403
Given an agreed behavior
and a bounded design,

54
00:02:26,943 --> 00:02:29,723
the agent can edit the relevant handler, add

55
00:02:29,723 --> 00:02:31,863
the data operation, and write checks.

56
00:02:32,743 --> 00:02:35,223
It should have room to
solve ordinary details.

57
00:02:35,863 --> 00:02:37,843
We do not need a meeting about every

58
00:02:37,843 --> 00:02:38,663
variable name.

59
00:02:39,143 --> 00:02:40,603
We do need it to preserve the permission

60
00:02:40,603 --> 00:02:42,183
rule while solving those details.

61
00:02:44,517 --> 00:02:48,097
Acceptance asks whether the
delivered change meets the

62
00:02:48,097 --> 00:02:49,037
agreed conditions.

63
00:02:49,697 --> 00:02:52,257
The agent can collect
evidence and help review

64
00:02:52,257 --> 00:02:52,657
a diff.

65
00:02:53,257 --> 00:02:55,777
The person or team
responsible for the release

66
00:02:55,777 --> 00:02:58,097
decides whether that evidence is sufficient.

67
00:02:58,837 --> 00:03:01,817
That responsibility is
easiest to exercise when the

68
00:03:01,817 --> 00:03:04,477
change is understandable
and the results are tied

69
00:03:04,477 --> 00:03:05,877
to specific requirements.

70
00:03:06,477 --> 00:03:08,757
A cheerful completion
message is a useful update,

71
00:03:08,877 --> 00:03:09,937
not the acceptance record.

72
00:03:12,283 --> 00:03:14,863
Here is the request we actually send in

73
00:03:14,863 --> 00:03:15,163
Cursor.

74
00:03:15,763 --> 00:03:18,623
We describe the future
approval feature, then ask

75
00:03:18,623 --> 00:03:21,623
for three things before
any code, what exists

76
00:03:21,623 --> 00:03:22,203
today,

77
00:03:23,163 --> 00:03:25,663
unresolved product decisions,
and checks we would need.

78
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:28,423
We name the server and the database test.

79
00:03:29,043 --> 00:03:31,483
For this investigation, we ask for no edits

80
00:03:31,483 --> 00:03:32,503
and no commands.

81
00:03:32,923 --> 00:03:35,663
That keeps the first step focused on reading

82
00:03:35,663 --> 00:03:36,563
and diagnosis.

83
00:03:39,017 --> 00:03:42,737
Cursor identifies the current
application as a read-only

84
00:03:42,737 --> 00:03:44,777
baseline with no approval path.

85
00:03:45,317 --> 00:03:48,857
It then separates existing
behavior, open decisions, and

86
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:49,617
possible checks.

87
00:03:50,277 --> 00:03:52,317
That is a productive use of an agent.

88
00:03:52,777 --> 00:03:54,597
It helps us discover the shape of the

89
00:03:54,597 --> 00:03:56,377
work before committing to a design.

90
00:03:57,177 --> 00:03:59,737
Now we verify the observations and make the

91
00:03:59,737 --> 00:04:02,437
choices, rather than
treating the response as an

92
00:04:02,437 --> 00:04:03,397
approved specification.

93
00:04:06,150 --> 00:04:07,790
Look at the actual server.

94
00:04:08,370 --> 00:04:10,330
There is a health route, a route that

95
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:13,410
lists synthetic requests,
and fallback error handling.

96
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:16,750
The list includes a tenant
identifier, but this

97
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:19,270
file does not enforce a
tenant permission boundary.

98
00:04:19,870 --> 00:04:22,490
A field named tenant does
not implement tenant

99
00:04:22,490 --> 00:04:22,990
isolation.

100
00:04:23,850 --> 00:04:25,410
We have a starting point for the course,

101
00:04:25,750 --> 00:04:27,910
and we should describe that
starting point accurately.

102
00:04:30,483 --> 00:04:34,183
The existing database test
opens a transaction, inserts

103
00:04:34,183 --> 00:04:36,903
a synthetic request,
rolls it back, and checks

104
00:04:36,903 --> 00:04:38,603
that the inserted row is gone.

105
00:04:39,223 --> 00:04:41,843
That tells us something
useful about database access

106
00:04:41,843 --> 00:04:42,623
and rollback.

107
00:04:43,123 --> 00:04:45,343
It does not call an approval endpoint or

108
00:04:45,343 --> 00:04:46,723
exercise a manager role.

109
00:04:47,303 --> 00:04:49,563
If this test passes after we add approval

110
00:04:49,563 --> 00:04:51,723
code, we still need evidence for the new

111
00:04:51,723 --> 00:04:52,043
behavior.

112
00:04:54,583 --> 00:04:57,603
The agent raises useful
questions about who may

113
00:04:57,603 --> 00:05:01,963
approve, valid state transitions,
audit records, tenant scope,

114
00:05:02,343 --> 00:05:04,123
repeated approvals, and the interface.

115
00:05:04,903 --> 00:05:06,803
These questions are
candidates for a decision.

116
00:05:07,423 --> 00:05:10,303
Some belong to product
policy, others are technical

117
00:05:10,303 --> 00:05:11,423
choices we can delegate.

118
00:05:12,203 --> 00:05:15,063
Sorting them prevents an
implementation detail from silently

119
00:05:15,063 --> 00:05:16,303
becoming a business rule.

120
00:05:16,703 --> 00:05:18,823
It also keeps an open
question from disappearing

121
00:05:18,823 --> 00:05:19,843
inside a long chat.

122
00:05:22,183 --> 00:05:24,543
Here is the brief prepared for our course

123
00:05:24,543 --> 00:05:25,083
project.

124
00:05:25,583 --> 00:05:28,563
Its status says proposed
behavior for a future

125
00:05:28,563 --> 00:05:29,203
implementation.

126
00:05:29,983 --> 00:05:31,363
Our choices are explicit,

127
00:05:32,043 --> 00:05:32,543
authentication,

128
00:05:33,243 --> 00:05:36,303
manager authority, a
matching tenant, and an open

129
00:05:36,303 --> 00:05:37,063
starting state.

130
00:05:37,663 --> 00:05:39,943
We also require an audit record for a

131
00:05:39,943 --> 00:05:40,943
successful transition.

132
00:05:41,663 --> 00:05:43,863
These are deliberate
choices for this example.

133
00:05:44,263 --> 00:05:46,843
A different product could
choose different behavior and

134
00:05:46,843 --> 00:05:47,783
need different evidence.

135
00:05:50,283 --> 00:05:52,603
Let us settle the repeated click case.

136
00:05:53,443 --> 00:05:56,163
Once the request is approved,
another approval attempt

137
00:05:56,163 --> 00:05:59,083
should leave the stored
state unchanged and create

138
00:05:59,083 --> 00:06:00,923
no second approval audit event.

139
00:06:01,503 --> 00:06:03,463
The response will explain
that the request is

140
00:06:03,463 --> 00:06:04,423
already approved.

141
00:06:05,163 --> 00:06:06,923
Notice how that describes a result we can

142
00:06:06,923 --> 00:06:07,283
observe.

143
00:06:07,783 --> 00:06:10,503
Later, we can design the
exact response contract

144
00:06:10,503 --> 00:06:13,523
and verify the behavior under
concurrent requests too.

145
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:19,390
For an unauthorized request,
the contract requires no

146
00:06:19,390 --> 00:06:22,810
state change and no successful
approval audit event.

147
00:06:23,210 --> 00:06:26,290
That does not forbid
recording a separate security

148
00:06:26,290 --> 00:06:26,770
event.

149
00:06:27,270 --> 00:06:29,050
It keeps two meanings distinct.

150
00:06:29,730 --> 00:06:32,430
We also need a policy for what information

151
00:06:32,430 --> 00:06:35,810
the response reveals about
a request in another

152
00:06:35,810 --> 00:06:36,170
tenant.

153
00:06:36,710 --> 00:06:38,590
We record that as a design question to

154
00:06:38,590 --> 00:06:41,750
resolve before implementation,
instead of guessing a status

155
00:06:41,750 --> 00:06:42,350
code here.

156
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:47,570
Consider two designs for
the state change and

157
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:48,370
its audit event.

158
00:06:49,150 --> 00:06:51,970
Separate writes are
straightforward to sketch, but one

159
00:06:51,970 --> 00:06:53,790
can succeed while the other fails.

160
00:06:54,490 --> 00:06:57,030
A transaction can keep
the two database changes

161
00:06:57,030 --> 00:06:57,510
together.

162
00:06:58,070 --> 00:07:02,110
That does not automatically solve
identity, permissions, retries,

163
00:07:02,290 --> 00:07:02,950
or concurrency.

164
00:07:03,770 --> 00:07:06,190
It answers one consistency question.

165
00:07:06,790 --> 00:07:10,010
Good architectural reasoning
names the property an option

166
00:07:10,010 --> 00:07:12,330
provides and the problems that remain.

167
00:07:14,883 --> 00:07:17,703
Our delegation now has a useful boundary.

168
00:07:18,383 --> 00:07:20,923
Explore an approach using
the existing service and

169
00:07:20,923 --> 00:07:21,323
database.

170
00:07:21,983 --> 00:07:25,543
Explain the authorization point,
the state transition, the

171
00:07:25,543 --> 00:07:26,963
audit write, and the checks.

172
00:07:27,723 --> 00:07:30,163
If the approach requires a new service, a

173
00:07:30,163 --> 00:07:33,323
new dependency with meaningful
operational cost, or a

174
00:07:33,323 --> 00:07:36,123
policy change, bring that
decision back with reasons.

175
00:07:36,803 --> 00:07:40,223
Routine implementation details
can stay inside the delegated

176
00:07:40,223 --> 00:07:40,583
work.

177
00:07:42,883 --> 00:07:46,063
Cursor has a planning
workflow for researching the

178
00:07:46,063 --> 00:07:48,303
code base and preparing a plan that you

179
00:07:48,303 --> 00:07:50,303
can inspect and edit before building.

180
00:07:50,883 --> 00:07:52,763
We will work through
that interface in detail

181
00:07:52,763 --> 00:07:53,183
later.

182
00:07:53,923 --> 00:07:55,123
The habit matters here.

183
00:07:55,723 --> 00:07:58,203
Review a plan against the
behavior and constraints.

184
00:07:58,963 --> 00:08:01,543
A plan earns confidence
by addressing the hard

185
00:08:01,543 --> 00:08:01,943
cases.

186
00:08:01,943 --> 00:08:04,683
Its length and formatting do not make the

187
00:08:04,683 --> 00:08:06,223
unresolved questions disappear.

188
00:08:08,483 --> 00:08:11,203
Now, map each requirement to evidence.

189
00:08:11,883 --> 00:08:14,563
A same tenant manager
approves an open request.

190
00:08:15,203 --> 00:08:17,543
Verify the stored state
and its audit record.

191
00:08:18,163 --> 00:08:20,423
An unauthorized actor attempts approval.

192
00:08:21,103 --> 00:08:23,423
Verify the refusal and unchanged state.

193
00:08:24,023 --> 00:08:25,423
A repeated approval arrives.

194
00:08:26,083 --> 00:08:28,743
Verify that it creates no
duplicate transition event.

195
00:08:29,443 --> 00:08:32,403
Each row names behavior and an observation.

196
00:08:32,403 --> 00:08:36,003
That makes a missing check
visible before somebody

197
00:08:36,003 --> 00:08:37,463
declares the feature finished.

198
00:08:40,017 --> 00:08:41,317
Here's a tempting shortcut.

199
00:08:41,777 --> 00:08:44,457
The happy path test passes, so approvals are

200
00:08:44,457 --> 00:08:44,897
secure.

201
00:08:45,437 --> 00:08:47,637
The observation is narrower
than that conclusion.

202
00:08:48,137 --> 00:08:50,797
A test can pass because
the implementation and

203
00:08:50,797 --> 00:08:53,357
the test share the same mistaken assumption.

204
00:08:53,937 --> 00:08:56,297
Review the expectation
against the requirement.

205
00:08:56,877 --> 00:08:58,657
Then look for a case that would expose

206
00:08:58,657 --> 00:08:59,257
the mistake.

207
00:08:59,637 --> 00:09:01,617
In this example, try a different tenant and

208
00:09:01,617 --> 00:09:02,797
a non-manager identity.

209
00:09:05,150 --> 00:09:08,710
A useful completion report
links the requirement, the

210
00:09:08,710 --> 00:09:12,010
changed files, the command
or interaction that checked

211
00:09:12,010 --> 00:09:13,690
it, and the observed result.

212
00:09:13,950 --> 00:09:16,290
It also states what remains untested.

213
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:19,030
When a check fails, keep the failure and

214
00:09:19,030 --> 00:09:20,790
explain the next investigation.

215
00:09:21,270 --> 00:09:24,190
That gives the next reviewer
something concrete to

216
00:09:24,190 --> 00:09:24,570
assess.

217
00:09:25,010 --> 00:09:26,910
It also lets an agent continue from durable

218
00:09:26,910 --> 00:09:29,530
evidence instead of reconstructing
the task from memory.

219
00:09:31,883 --> 00:09:34,023
Take a moment with the exercise and pause

220
00:09:34,023 --> 00:09:34,863
when you need to.

221
00:09:35,343 --> 00:09:36,483
Sort these four decisions.

222
00:09:37,243 --> 00:09:37,903
Approval policy,

223
00:09:38,603 --> 00:09:41,003
consistency between the
status and its audit record,

224
00:09:41,443 --> 00:09:44,683
the name of a local helper, and acceptance

225
00:09:44,683 --> 00:09:45,863
of the finished feature.

226
00:09:46,423 --> 00:09:49,823
For each decision, identify
the responsible role, then

227
00:09:49,823 --> 00:09:51,823
describe what the agent
can contribute and what

228
00:09:51,823 --> 00:09:53,503
information the responsible person needs.

229
00:10:01,983 --> 00:10:05,363
Approval policy belongs with
the people accountable for

230
00:10:05,363 --> 00:10:06,243
the product behavior.

231
00:10:07,083 --> 00:10:10,663
Consistency is an architectural
responsibility informed by that

232
00:10:10,663 --> 00:10:11,103
policy.

233
00:10:11,863 --> 00:10:14,243
A helper name can usually
be delegated within

234
00:10:14,243 --> 00:10:15,443
the code base's conventions.

235
00:10:16,363 --> 00:10:19,283
Acceptance belongs to the
responsible reviewer or release

236
00:10:19,283 --> 00:10:21,503
owner supported by the collected evidence.

237
00:10:22,183 --> 00:10:23,663
These roles can overlap.

238
00:10:24,243 --> 00:10:27,063
The point is to make authority visible, not

239
00:10:27,063 --> 00:10:29,303
to invent a committee for a small change.

240
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:34,790
We now have a clearer task, a baseline

241
00:10:34,790 --> 00:10:39,190
we inspected, proposed behavior
we chose, technical questions

242
00:10:39,190 --> 00:10:42,370
we recorded, a scope for delegation, and an

243
00:10:42,370 --> 00:10:43,070
evidence map.

244
00:10:43,750 --> 00:10:46,310
The feature is still unbuilt, and the brief

245
00:10:46,310 --> 00:10:48,750
tells us exactly what must happen next.

246
00:10:49,430 --> 00:10:51,390
In the next lesson, we will turn these

247
00:10:51,390 --> 00:10:53,450
responsibilities into a working loop.

248
00:10:54,350 --> 00:10:58,090
Understand, plan, build,
verify, and integrate.
